<h1>Hard Choices in a Dangerous World: Disappeared</h1>
 
<p class="post-intro">
A reporter chasing the truth. A brother chasing easy money. And a city where both choices
could get you killed.
</p>
 
<div class="post-body">
 
<p>
Pretty much this story is about Sara Zapata, whose best friend has been kidnapped by
criminals in Juarez, Mexico. Sara works as a reporter and makes use of her job by drawing
attention to girls who have been kidnapped in the city. As publicity increased, Sara and
her family started getting threatened. Meanwhile, her younger brother Emiliano is being
tempted into the drug trade by the promise of easy money. This leads to uncanny
circumstances that they have to go through, and eventually the two of them have no choice
but to risk crossing the desert into the United States to stay safe.
</p>
 
<h2>Two Siblings, Two Journeys</h2>
<p>
The author alternates between the two siblings' perspectives chapter by chapter. Sara's
perspective moves fast and drives the plot, as she investigates and exposes the criminals.
Emiliano's is more focused on his internal conflict — his temptation to join the "web" for
money, and his feelings for Perla Rubi, a girl he likes.
</p>
 
<h2>Hard Choices</h2>
<p>
The characters go through hard choices during this time of their lives. Sara has to choose
between staying safe and continuing to expose the truth about the kidnappings. While Sara
has to do that, Emiliano has to choose between easy money or staying out of the criminal
web and doing the right thing. Both siblings end up choosing family and each other over
everything else, even when it means leaving their home country.
</p>
 
<div class="highlight-box">
I really do like how the author introduces real world issues like danger to journalists,
corruption, and how people get pulled into crime.
</div>
 
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>
Overall, I really enjoyed this book and I think the ending was satisfying without being
too predictable. I liked reading Sara's chapters a little more because of how fast paced
and suspenseful they were, but Emiliano's struggle felt more real and relatable in a
different way. One part that really stuck with me was seeing how easily someone could get
pulled into something dangerous just because of the promise of money.
</p>
 
<p>
I'd recommend this book to anyone who likes thrillers or true crime type stories.
</p>
 
